  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-2-11\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=-2,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=-11,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}+(-11)^{2} \\\\  & =4+121 \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-2-11\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{\u2015}{z}=-2+11\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =(-2-11\\text{i})(-2+11\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}+(-2)(11)\\text{i}+(-11)(-2)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(11)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\cancel{22\\text{i}}+\\cancel{22\\text{i}}-(-1)(121) \\\\  & =25+100 \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
